{"causes":["Plato was inspired by earlier Greek philosophical traditions","The dialogues of Plato were heavily influenced by his teacher Socrates' methodological approach"],"effects":["Influenced the development of Western moral philosophy","Shaped the intellectual landscape of ancient Greece"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"socrates","sourceName":"Socrates","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"euthyphro","targetName":"Euthyphro","context":"In Euthyphro, Socrates engages in a critical examination of traditional Athenian religiosity"},{"sourceSlug":"philosophy","sourceName":"Philosophy","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"western-thought","targetName":"Western Thought","context":"Euthyphro contributed to the development of Western philosophical thought"},{"sourceSlug":"greece","sourceName":"Greece","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"athens","targetName":"Athens","context":"The dialogue was composed in Athens, where it reflects on the city's religious and cultural practices"},{"sourceSlug":"plato","sourceName":"Plato","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"eutyphro","targetName":"Euthyphro","context":"The text is attributed to Plato, one of the most influential philosophers in Western history"},{"sourceSlug":"greece-4th-century-bce","sourceName":"Ancient Greece (4th century BCE)","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"eutyphro","targetName":"Euthyphro","context":"The dialogue is set in ancient Athens during the 4th century BCE"}],"places":[{"name":"Athens","role":"Birthplace and Setting"},{"name":"Ancient Greece","role":"Geographical Context"},{"name":"Delphi","role":"Place of Significance"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Euthyphro","type":"Reference article","slug":"euthyphro"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q648889","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euthyphro"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Euthyphro contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Euthyphro is a Socratic dialogue by Plato (c. 428–348 BCE), composed c. 399 BCE (shortly before the trial of Socrates, to which the dialogue's framing refers) — one of the most philosophically significant short dialogues in the Platonic corpus, and the locus classicus of a problem that has remained at the centre of the philosophy of religion for more than two millennia: the Euthyphro dilemma. The dialogue is set on the porch of the King-Archon in Athens, where Socrates encounters the seer Euthyphro — who is prosecuting his own father for impiety (having left a servant to die bound in a ditch while Euthyphro sought religious guidance) — and engages him in a discussion of the nature of piety (Greek: hosion) and impiety. Euthyphro offers several definitions of piety that Socrates systematically refutes, and the dialogue concludes without a successful definition — a classic aporetic ending.

The philosophical core of the Euthyphro is the dilemma Socrates poses at 10a: 'Is the pious beloved by the gods because it is pious, or is it pious because it is beloved by the gods?' — the Euthyphro dilemma. This question, in its theological form, asks whether what God commands is good because God commands it (the Divine Command Theory) or whether God commands what is good because it is independently good — a dilemma that strikes at the foundations of theistic ethics. If the former (Divine Command Theory), then morality is arbitrary — God could in principle command torture, and it would be good. If the latter, then goodness is independent of God's will, and God is constrained by an independent standard of goodness — apparently compromising divine omnipotence or making God unnecessary to ethics. This dilemma has been debated by theologians and philosophers from the medieval scholastics (Aquinas vs. Ockham) through Leibniz and Spinoza to contemporary metaethics.

The Euthyphro also serves as an introduction to Plato's theory of Forms — Socrates's demand for a single definition of piety that applies to all pious actions is his characteristic demand for the Form (the essence, the universal) rather than a list of examples — and as a portrait of Socratic method applied to a practical moral and religious problem in a recognisably Athenian context.","subjects":["Philosophy","Classical Era","Plato","Socratic Dialogues","Ancient Greece","Philosophy of Religion","Ethics","Greek Philosophy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Euthyphro's exploration of piety and justice has had a lasting impact on Western moral philosophy, shaping debates about the nature of divine command theory.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
